#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049053 :topic/id #uuid "d5efe33b-5861-5f40-bd4b-3f871de6b84d" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110494 :question/id #uuid "7c937587-dc81-5ee9-aa66-a509a87433e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4067" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation $2^{x+1}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right) - 1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f4b65e-b93a-51e8-b943-b3b53d47fffb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110594 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110495 :question/id #uuid "45d74b50-fd9d-5d18-835d-a7e140278a34" :question/math-academy-id "e-4069" :question/problem "Given that $3^{2x+3}=5,$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Taking $\\log_3$ of both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n   \t3^{2x+3} \t\t\t\t&= 5 \\\\[3pt]\n\t\\log_3 \\left(3^{2x+3}\\right)\t&= \\log_3 (5) \\\\[3pt]\n\t2x+3 \t\t\t\t\t&= \\log_3 (5) . \\\\[3pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx -0.768\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n**Note:** We used the change of base formula to rewrite the right-hand side, as many calculators can only compute base $10$ logarithms."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7784d8-f6a4-5e45-b03e-d76a27372c48" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110595 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110496 :question/id #uuid "5d31a3e8-aae6-50b5-9ce1-65cef1d8395e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4071" :question/problem "Given that $3\\cdot 6^{x} = 9,$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {6}^{x} & =9 \\\\ {6}^{x} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take $\\log_{6}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n   \t6^x \t\t\t\t\t&= 3 \\\\[3pt]\n\t\\log_6 \\left(6^x\\right)\t&= \\log_6 (3) \\\\[3pt]\n\tx \t\t\t\t\t&= \\log_6 (3)\\\\[3pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\log(3)}{\\log(6)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.613\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d076e5d-9d08-5355-b813-d1749feb0803" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1482 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050649}] :topic/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049589 :topic/id #uuid "02b0bd28-fe3c-5e27-b64f-dcec4176a6ea" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087683 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087491 :question/id #uuid "effc520d-4717-5e46-9358-c2ea34c7aa24" :question/math-academy-id "e-3990" :question/problem "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, what is the value of $\\dfrac{4}{\\ln(2)}?$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the required expression to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} & ≈\\frac{4}{0.693\\,15} \\\\  & ≈5.770\\,76 \\\\  & ≈5.77.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d82c39c-5ec6-5cf3-96f8-e778091a451a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Natural Logarithms Using a Calculator"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087684 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087492 :question/id #uuid "97f9f0d6-d948-5f9b-b0ae-57e6a456a53b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4056" :question/problem "Convert the expression $\\ln{3} \\approx 1.1$ to its equivalent exponential form." :question/worked-solution "The natural logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\ln{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = e^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\ln{3} \\approx 1.1$ with $\\ln{a} = x,$ we get $a = 3$ and $x = 1.1.$\n\nTherefore, the approximation $\\ln{3} \\approx 1.1$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\ne^{1.1} \\approx 3 .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30497207-86c5-507f-906a-d23bd0af71e9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Exponential and Logarithmic Form With Natural Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087685 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087493 :question/id #uuid "fd02e00f-9e2c-54f1-a5d3-fb0da9c9a413" :question/math-academy-id "e-4057" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\ln\\left( e^{6}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The natural logarithm of a power of $e$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\ln(e^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = 6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( e^{6}\\right) = 6.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a323ad55-d029-58f4-b0a3-cc10b7dab125" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087692 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087497 :question/id #uuid "37ca3b87-9f5f-583c-95ff-83ca35608559" :question/math-academy-id "e-4155" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{e^{3}}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\dfrac{1}{e^3}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{e^3} = e^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( \\dfrac{1}{e^3} \\right) = \\ln \\left( e^{-3} \\right).\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm of a power of $e$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\ln(e^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( e^{-3}\\right) = - 3.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "169ca81a-c698-553c-9f47-d6c4303b82a9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"}] :topic/math-academy-id 818 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050732}] :topic/title "The Natural Logarithm"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049879 :topic/id #uuid "1a5322f4-e5da-55f5-a61c-018264a8843c" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110596 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110497 :question/id #uuid "bf0e781a-8bf7-51c8-a67b-031449fee038" :question/math-academy-id "e-4078" :question/problem "By expressing $\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)$ in the form $k\\ln x,$ determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k =2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddcd6d98-7aad-5d57-9a17-cc05cbce863a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying the Logarithm of a Power Function Using the Power Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110597 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110498 :question/id #uuid "53a33ca5-8a37-5ec7-acb2-00e3471a1c6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4079" :question/problem "By expressing $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_3{x}$ in the form $\\log_3 \\left(x^k\\right),$ determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡x & ={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6657c93d-ca62-5bce-a136-b2e8a8c43869" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression as the Logarithm of a Power Function Using the Power Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110598 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110499 :question/id #uuid "7e755fc4-40b5-5d64-8087-ae67baad204c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4080" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\ln\\left(x^3\\right)$ if $\\ln(x) = 0.2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( c^m \\right) = m\\ln{c}\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}) & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0a4cb5a-4492-56df-8138-0d8cb9a07d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Using the Power Rule"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1475 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125}] :topic/title "The Power Rule for Logarithms"}]}
